    static let paragraphs = [
        "People have folded the things they read for as long as they have written on surfaces too large to carry. A sheet that opens wide on a table has to close small enough for a pocket, and the crease that makes that possible is always a compromise between the two shapes.",
        "Road maps show the compromise most plainly. The cartographer draws one continuous picture, the printer cuts it into panels, and the reader learns to keep a finger on a town while the fold runs straight through the road they need. Nobody designs a map around its creases; the creases are simply where the paper gives way.",
        "Books took the other path. Once pages were bound along one edge, the fold stopped being an accident and became the spine. Text was set in two blocks, one on each side, and the gutter between them grew wide enough that no line ever had to cross it. Readers stopped noticing the fold because the layout had already moved out of its way.",
        "Newspapers kept both habits at once. A broadsheet folds across the middle for the stand and down the centre for the train, and editors learned to place headlines above the horizontal fold so that the paper sold itself when it lay folded on a pile. Everything below the fold was for people who had already decided to read.",
        "Letters folded into envelopes taught a quieter lesson: the reader opens the sheet in a fixed order, and the writer can count on it. The greeting sits in the first panel to be seen, the signature in the last, and nothing important is placed where the paper bends twice and wears thin.",
        "A pocket notebook is the opposite case. It is meant to be held half open, one page for the eye and one for the hand, and a good one lies flat without being pressed. Its two pages are never one surface; they are two surfaces that happen to share a binding.",
        "Accordion books ask the reader to choose. Opened a few panels at a time they read like a book; pulled out to full length they become a single strip that runs across a whole table. The same object supports both, but only because every panel was designed to stand on its own.",
        "Gatefold record sleeves were built to be opened once and admired. The artwork crossed the fold on purpose, and people forgave the crease because they were looking at the picture from a distance, not reading it closely. Close reading and spectacle want different things from a fold.",
        "Field guides are held open in one hand, often in the wind, and their designers learned early to keep the plate on one page and the description on the other. Nobody wants to read a bird's markings across the spine while the bird is still on the branch.",
        "Railway timetables were the hardest case. Long tables of numbers had to survive folding, and a misread column meant a missed train. Printers repeated the station names on both sides of every fold so that a reader never had to trace a row across a crease.",
        "Tri-fold leaflets are small enough that every panel is read in turn, but large enough that the order matters. The panel seen first when the leaflet lies on a counter is the one that has to explain why anyone should pick it up.",
        "Atlases settled the matter for good. When a region was too wide for one page, the mapmaker either repeated a strip along the gutter or broke the map at a river or a border, somewhere the eye already expected a pause. The fold was respected, not ignored.",
        "The lesson across all of these is the same. A fold is not a defect in the surface; it is a fact about how the surface is held. Layouts that work with it put a pause where the paper bends, keep each side meaningful on its own, and leave continuous reading to flow down a page rather than across the crease.",
        "A folding phone inherits every one of these habits at once. Held closed it is a pocket notebook, open on a table it is an atlas, and propped on its hinge it is a newspaper lying folded on a stand. The layouts that read best on it are the ones that already knew where paper bends.",
    ]
}
